net/sched: sch_cake: clamp the TCP window of flows queueing on ingress

Ported from mindwolf80/cake-wnd @ ef18fc80da ("sch_cake: carry upstream
locking, backlog and overhead fixes"). Its base is Linux 6.12.112 plus the
sch_cake.c hunks of the v7.0 cake_mq backports (700-02/03/04/05/07 and
701-01/02), so it already carries the cake_mq kind: replacing our file with
it would keep cake_mq and lose only this tree's 999-71x debugfs layer
(tin_share, tin_cap, sync_time_ns, rebalance), which it does not have.

Grafted additively instead, for two reasons: to keep that 999-71x layer, and
to avoid re-taking a 6.12.112 file up to 6.18.56. The clamp block is
self-contained -- it works on the skb alone, no cake_sched_data access -- and
the three hooks only test CAKE_FLAG_INGRESS, which this tree already reaches
through q->config->rate_flags: the same shape cake-wnd uses after the same
backport, so the block is taken verbatim.

An ingress-mode CAKE instance flags a TCP flow when COBALT drops (or
CE-marks) one of its packets, i.e. a standing queue rather than a burst; any
egress-mode instance then lowers the window advertised in that flow's ACKs.
While a flow stays flagged its clamp is cut by 1/4 once per CAKE interval,
down to wnd_min_bytes; when the flag expires the clamp is raised by 1/4 per
interval until it reaches the advertised window. Off by default: wnd_clamp=0
leaves cake byte-for-byte identical to stock, so this patch is inert until
asked for.

No effect on this box as configured: no CAKE instance drops (ifb4eth1 is
capped at 5Gbit, above the ~2.3Gbit the line delivers; eth0 is unlimited)
and the actual limiter is the QDMA hardware scheduler, invisible to CAKE.
Arming it means giving an ingress cake a rate below the line -- see the
qos-mt6000 qdisc option.

